ly leaves  people who manage to climb their way up  you can see all these ideas of the  underground in the very last shot of the  film  after the sun vows to get rich and save  his father from the bunker we get the  same pedestal shot from the beginning no  matter how hard he tries or how much he  plans he remains trapped underground  the underground isn't just a physical  space in this film it's a social  boundary policed by the norms and  manners of an upper-class society with  no interest in admitting anyone new  in parasite there is no meritocracy the  hatch of the underground is locked and  sealed  basements aren't just scary because  they're cold and dark they're scary  because of their power as a storytelling  device  in film and tv the underground  represents a new unexplored space that  may be a source of safety  danger or reward this unknown creates  dramatic tension because  it's so unknown when we see a basement  we're introduced to a completely new  space and we have no idea what's going  to be down there waiting for us  in a way almost every underground space  in film and tv yields something valuable  they yield the most valuable part of the  story so the next time you see a  basement in a film  don't forget to consider how deep it  really goes  like most now you see it videos this one  only contains a fraction of all the  thoughts and ideas we had around this  topic but we want to let you in on the  deeper conversations we had about the  underground breaking bad  and parasite we do that and more in our  latest episode of the now you hear it  podcast  the now you hear it podcast goes in  depth on each month's topic expands our  thoughts and discusses related topics  that didn't make it into the video  you can get exclusive access to our past  two episodes plus our brand new episode  on the underground by going to  patreon.com now you see it and becoming  a five dollar tier member and if you  want to check out our first  episode for free the link is in 